///! process: subprocess spawning.
///!
///! Build a `Command`, call `spawn`, manage a `Child` with
///! captured stdin/stdout/stderr. POSIX uses `fork + execve`; Windows
///! uses `CreateProcessA` and is supported with known gaps (see "Windows
///! limitations" below).
///!
///! Between fork and exec the child runs a small amount of code:
///! allocator calls, only async-signal-safe libc. It then either execs
///! the target or `_exit(127)` on failure. The parent sees 127 as
///! "exec failed" regardless of why.
///!
///! In scope:
///! - `Command` with program / args / env overrides / cwd
///! - `Stdio::Inherit / Null / Piped / Fd(i32)`
///! - `Child::wait` / `join` / `try_wait` / `kill` / `send_signal`
///! - `Command::output` / `status` / `spawn`
///! - Captured `ChildStdin` / `ChildStdout` / `ChildStderr` that
///!   implement `Write` / `Read`
///!
///! Async: `Child::join` awaits the child's exit without stalling an
///! executor worker - a pidfd on the reactor where the kernel has one, a
///! `BlockingPool` thread everywhere else (`child::ChildWait` documents the
///! split). Everything else here is still synchronous.
///!
///! Out of scope for now:
///! - Async pipe I/O: the captured `ChildStdout` / `ChildStderr` reads and
///!   the `Command::output` / `status` wrappers still block. Windows pipes
///!   are not sockets, so they cannot be armed on that platform's reactor;
///!   the pool is their route when they land.
///! - Detached / double-fork daemonization
///! - Process groups / session leader control / Windows job objects
///!
///! Windows (`CreateProcessA`) - supported; the gaps below remain:
///! - `Stdio::Inherit / Null / Piped / Fd` all wired; `env_clear` /
///!   per-variable env overrides and `cwd` are applied
///! - `output` interleaves the stdout/stderr drains via `PeekNamedPipe`,
///!   so a child that fills one pipe buffer first does not deadlock
///! - `send_signal` beyond `kill` is POSIX-only (no Windows signal model)

namespace std::process;

import std::ffi::libc;

public module process::signal;
public module process::child;
public module process::command;
public module process::exec;

/// The current process's OS process id (pid).  On POSIX this is
/// `getpid()`; on Windows it is `GetCurrentProcessId()`, both surfaced
/// through `libc::getpid`.
function id() -> i32 {
    return libc::getpid();
}
